  "summary": "Edo-period Japanese woodblock prints of actors, courtesans, landscapes and urban pleasures, defined by flat color, bold line and asymmetry.",
  "description": "Ukiyo-e — \"pictures of the floating world\" — mass-produced images of Kabuki actors, beauties, famous places and folk tales. Masters like Hokusai, Hiroshige, Utamaro and Sharaku developed flat color fields, strong contour lines, dramatic cropping and stylized waves and clouds. Imported to Europe in the 19th century, it ignited Japonisme and reshaped Impressionism, Art Nouveau and modern graphic design. Its visual grammar — flat planes, outline, asymmetric composition — remains a foundation of Japanese visual identity.",
  "culturalContext": "Ukiyo-e — \"pictures of the floating world\" — was Edo Japan's mass visual culture (1670s-1860s): woodblock prints of actors (Sharaku), courtesans, landmarks (Hokusai's 36 Views, Hiroshige's Tokaido), and chonin town pleasure quarters, sold for the price of a bowl of noodles. Its prussian-blue breakthrough (1820s), flat planes, cropping and line confidence rewired Western art through Japonisme (Whistler, Van Gogh, Degas); today it remains the world's most recognized Japanese graphic tradition.",
